 ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu (David Friedman) writes:
>2. I do, however, have a prejudice against treating a guess as a fact,
>interpreting all further evidence with that fact as a given, and then
>claiming that the evidence so interpreted supports the fact. So far as
>consciousness is concerned, it seems to me that the mechanistic
>version--essentially the idea that I am a computer program running on my
>brain--is only a guess. Nobody I have talked to has a convincing story of
>how the most fundamental fact I know--the subjective experience of being
>conscious--is explained by a mechanism. Nobody has built a mechanism that
>acts as much like a conscious being as other people do.

 We have pieces of what we think a mechanistic explanation would be, which
explain parts of human behavior. I know of no comparable claim about
non-mechanistic "theories".

>Of course, the various non-mechanism theories do no better. Nobody has
>offered me an adequate nonmechanistic explanation of consciousness, and I
>have not met any golems yet. We do have a method of producing conscious
>beings, but it (fortunately) does not depend on our understanding how they
>work.

 I have a prejudice against classifying something a theory when I can't
understand it. I think the non-mechanism "theory" says either
 A) X will prevent us from understanding the mind, or
 B) the time T between now and when we understand the mind is infinity.
As long as X remains undefined (or is defined as an entity such as god
or the soul whose attributes are not testable), I will deny that A is
a scientific alternative to a mechanistic theory. Since I can conceive
of very many possible values of T and can't identify anything unique about
T = infinity, I see nothing remarkable about assigning a low enough value
to P(T = infinity) that I can treat it as zero for most purposes. I know
that I don't have a compelling reason for treating infinity as if it were
just another value of T, but I can't see why your apparent belief that
the P(T = infinity) should be treated as comparable to P(0 < T < infinity)
is any less arbitrary.

