Date: Tue, 25 May 93 21:05:39 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu

> 4. Much of this goes back to an argument I had with Minski, who was
> claiming that he knew the mind was a program because that was proved by the
> gedanken experiment of uploading one neuron at a time (most people on the
> list are probably familiar with the argument). My response was that he had
> not proved his result because he had not done the experiment--he was merely
> concluding that it would go the way he thought because he already knew the
> right answer, and then taking the fact that it would go that way as
> evidence that his answer was right.

Suppose you simulated a physical system at a sufficiently low level to
reproduce its actual behavior.  Suppose the physical system is a brain.
The simulated brain will act in the same way as a real brain.  It will
claim to be conscious, because the real brain does.  It will argue and
debate and plead its case, because the real brain does.

Denying the consciousness of a debating opponent who speaks and acts as
a conscious being and who vehemently asserts his consciousness is an untenable
act of solipsism.  Therefore we must accept that computers running programs
can be conscious.

To deny this conclusion, one must either assert that accurate simulation
of physical systems is inherently impossible, or one must be willing to
deny the consciousness of others who argue as convincingly as humans that
they are conscious.

Hal

