Date: Tue, 25 May 93 23:41:07 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

It is worth looking at the experience of a previous attempt on a slightly
smaller scale. The Terry Anderson/P.G. Hill analysis of homesteading
suggests that the result is to dissipate the value of the land in the cost
of premature settlement intended to establish property rights, and that the
solution is an auction along the general lines that Robin suggests. They
did not consider a single auction of everything--on the other hand, optimal
bundling may have been less a problem for a relatively familiar resource
such as land.

What is interesting about the story is that their suggestion was
tried--first. The Homestead act was only passed in the early 1860's (1862 I
think). For the preceding seventy or eighty years the states and then the
Federal government tried to auction off the land, with a notable lack of
success.

The problem comes from auctioning an inhomogeneous resource owned by a
political organization. It took two typical forms. Both start when the
government announces that a region previously closed to settlement has just
been opened and is about to be auctioned off. On careful inspection, it is
discovered that the empty land  has people living on it--"squatters" who
have started their settlement before it became legal.

The squatters protest to congress that they are patriotic frontiersmen, out
expanding the borders of the nation, and that they should therefore be
given a special opportunity, just this once, to buy their land at the
minimum legal price instead of having to bid for it in an auction.
Squatters are also voters, so sometimes congress gives in.

Sometimes congress does not give in, and announces an auction. The auction
must be held near the land, since buyers have to inspect it before they
know what to bid. You are a Boston citizen, representing a group of land
investors, and have come out to some courthouse in the far west--say what
will be eastern Ohio--for the auction. You observe that the courthouse is
filled with rough, tough, frontiersmen with knives in their belts. They
politely explain to you that bidding against a settler for his land is not
considered polite in these parts, and that the last fellow who did was
never seen again. You decide to restrict your bids to the unsettled
bits--which is to say, the least attractive. Oddly enough, when the auction
is held, almost all of the land goes for the minimum price.

A few more details. The squatters/settlers have each actually cleared and
made use of ten or twenty acres. Each of them buys, at the minimum legal
price, a 160 acre quarter section containing his cleared bit. After
celebrating their triumph over the evil Boston land speculators, they
resell the land at a market price to the next wave of settlers, pack up
their gear, and move west onto land still closed for settlement.

There was, as I remember, one transaction more like what Robin proposes. A
big chunk of land--I think in eastern Ohio, but it might have been western
pennsylvania or New York--was sold to one buyer, who later subdivided and
resold. My impression was that it worked a little better than the standard
method, but not enough better, at least from the standpoint of those making
the decisions, to become a model for future transactions.

All of this is a stylized, but I think fairly accurate, account of what I
got from reading a big book on the history of the public lands a few years
back. 

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


