This, for me, is the sticking point. My consciousness is the thing of which
I am most immediately aware. It seems obvious to me that a claim of the
form "all of the experimental evidence I have seen for scientific
proposition X was forged by some very clever madman," although very
implausible, is more plausible than the claim "All of that evidence is
true, and it implies that I do not exist because there is no such thing as
consciousness." This, I think, is the point of Descartes famous "proof" of
his own existence. I do not know that I (my consciousness) exists with
certainty, but I know it with more certainty than I know anything else,
since everything else is coming to me through my consciousness.

I am perfectly happy to accept the claim that my body is largely mechanism.
That claim has, I think, been accepted by most people, religious or
otherwise, in recent millenia (with some exceptions such as Christian
Scientists). Our present knowledge refines on it a good deal, but lots of
it--the understanding of bones, joints, etc.--goes way back. The
observation that my consciousness is affected by physical events is also
not new--it is what we mean by perception.

But it does not seem to me that anything we have learned takes us very far
towards explaining consciousness. That being the case, the answer to
Perry's question about where a soul could hide, how it alters the brain, is
that the soul (by which I mean some non-mechanistic explanation of what I
claim we have as yet no idea of a mechanistic explanation of) is providing
(or is) consciousness. I cannot offer a good explanation of how it does
this, or what it is, or how it interacts with the parts of the body we
understand, because I have no idea what a soul is--I am simply using the
term as shorthand to cover all explanations that are not epiphenomena of
mechanism. So we have a phenomenon--a self conscious being--for which we
have neither a non-mechanistic nor a mechanistic explanation. I conclude
that confidence that we are going to have one or the other is misplaced.

One reply which Perry could make, although I do not think he will, is to
deny that he understands what I mean by consciousness. As we all know,
definitions define some words in terms of other words, so by the time you
get down to sufficiently fundamental concepts you are unable to define them
in still more fundamental terms. You can still explain them by pointing--in
this case by referring to that characteristic of me which I am sure I have,
pretty sure a rock does not have, fairly sure other humans, dogs, cats,
have, which ....   .

The reply I think he is making is that he understands my puzzle, but
regards its solution as just one further step in a path we have already
travelled much of--a step we can pretty confident of taking one of these
years. My problem with this is that, so far as I can tell, people have been
wrestling with this particular problem for several thousand years and have
made strikingly little progress. I concede that familiarity with computer
programs represents a little progress, since it helps explain some of the
things associated with consciousness--but not much. I do not concede that
the understanding of nerve transmission, or what happens when you poke the
brain in various ways, represents significant progress in solving this
particular problem--it just means that we understand better the sorts of
things we have long taken for granted as mechanism.

