I certainly agree that the converse is not the same as the contrapositive.
My argument, however, is based on probability theory. It goes as follows:

Consider some experiment which has two possible outcomes A and B, and some
proposition X. 

Assume that outcome A is evidence for X, which means that if outcome A is
observed, our probability for the truth of X will be higher than before the
experiment.

Let PA be the ex ante probability of outcome A, PB of outcome B. PA+PB=1
Let PAX be the probability that X is true conditional on outcome A, PBX be
the probability that X is true conditional on outcome B. 

Prior to doing the experiment, the probability that X is true (P) is:

P=PA x PAX +PB x PBX

By our assumption, PAX>P. If PBX>=P, the rhs of the equation above is
greater than the lhs, which is impossible. So PBX<P. If the result of our
experiment is B, the probability that X is true becomes PBX. So failure of
the experiment lowers the probability that X is true. QED

Note that the conclusion does not hold if PB=0--if, in other words, we are
certain before the experiment that we will not get result B. But in that
case the assumption also does not hold. P=PA, so our probability for X does
not rise when we get result A, so A is not evidence for X. It also does not
hold if we are certain that X is true--because in that case PAX=PBX=1. In
that case again A is not evidence for X.


