>observation of a mechanistic brain seems to be all but proof of the
>assertion at this point. Unless you can give me an explanation of how
>a soul could possibly interact with the known mechanistic processes in
>such a way as to evade detection by researchers for all these years of
>the phenomenon, I'd have to argue that you are wrong.

And unless you can give me an explanation of how the known or conjectured
mechanistic processes of the brain could possibly give rise to the
phenomenon of self consciousness, ...  . My reasons for believing in my
consciousness are much stronger than my reasons for believing in the known
mechanistic processes of the brain.

On a slight tangent--I do not find the "in such a way as to evade detection
by researchers for all these years of the phenomenon" part of Perry's
argument very convincing. Existing mechanistic explanations do not, after
all, go far enough to actually predict what I will do under most
circumstances. So if something is interacting with the known processes in
such a way as to determine their outcome--the choices--there is no reason
to think we would notice. Of course, if the interaction was in the middle
of some process we were watching and understood fairly well we might spot
it--but I don't think our knowledge is good enough so that that condition
applies to much of the inside of our brains.


