Date: Wed, 26 May 1993 00:11:08 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: MEDIA: video games review

I recently had occasion to visit one of Chicago's finest video arcades, and
thought some of you might like to hear about some of (what were to me, at
least) the latest games.

They had several of the virtual reality machines that have been around for
some time now.  The name escapes me, but most of you have no doubt seen
photos of the set-up: you stand on a platform surrounded by a waist-high
ring, wear a helmet, and hold a gun.  You battle fellow players and green
pterodactyls.  The graphics are pretty jaggy, as the game is run on an
Amiga platform.  There are a few of these machines in UC Berkeley's student
union.

But this arcade had a new, sit-down version of the game.  I didn't get a
chance to play it, but it seems to be more of the same with different
software.

In a more conventional vein, I saw a video game first: side-by-side screens
for panoramic scenes.  The game "X-Men" offered this format.  Although the
split between the screens creates a small area of visual confusion, you
quickly forget this minor flaw.  It's a nice effect and a great game.

"F-15 Fighter Pilot" (or "F-15 somethingelse") offers one of the most
effective fake flights I've seen to date--and I have flown both real planes
and the popular "Flight Simulator."  This game has very good graphics and a
solid controls (many flight simulators are far too sensitive).  There is a
lot of visual stimuli, yet the basics are easy to master.  

"NBA Basketball" has some of the best representations of humans in action I
have ever seen.  You can really tell it's Scotty Pippen!  I didn't get a
chance to play this, but it looks like a real hoot.  This is one basketball
game where no one calls *any* fouls!  Pow! Ow! DUNK!

"Street Fighter II" has been around awhile, but remains one of the better
games in this genre.  I once played it in another arcade on a machine that
displayed the images via a projection TV-type set-up.  Intense!  This
arcade only offered the usual, small screen.  Itself an offspring of the
vastly inferior "Street Fighter," "Street Fighter II" has encouraged
several copycats, some of which have grown quite sophisticated.  Others are
gruesomely funny.  I saw one where a chainsaw-bearing character lost and
arm and continued swinging while his useless limb lay at his feet. 
Overtones of M. Python's "It's just a scratch!"

Lastly, I highly recommend "Virtual Racer" as the best auto-racing
simulation I have seen to date.  Unfortunately, this Chicago arcade didn't
have "VR."  I just gotta' find one, somewhere, fast!  

